#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166473 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ada3a84a-f979-5aee-9422-7acb8e430c11" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "9377994d-f08b-57ee-8bd5-2315bf74a70f" :question/math-academy-id "e-21632" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t) + 2y(t); y^{′}(t) = x(t) - 3y(t)$\nSketch the phase portrait of the system of linear differential equations above given that the eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$, respectively." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny= \\dfrac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/a3ea75c2afeb553be2bd71c6717e1fa0.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/cb8af2968caaacb5b676cea6316430ee.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n$\\lambda_2 < \\lambda_1 < 0$\nthus,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nis the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2 = -4\n$$\n\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/ccc3466bc4de9ad91d2f893096827841.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166475 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "1c7567cf-f861-5088-b43e-ff94fcff0c91" :question/math-academy-id "e-21634" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 9x(t) + 6y(t); y^{′}(t) =-6x(t) - 6y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1=6$ and $\\lambda_2=-3$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-2, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [-1, \\ 2]^T$, respectively. Complete the following statements about the system by filling in the blanks.\n\nIdentify stable and unstable lines, and classify the equilibrium point (origin)." :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -\\dfrac12x$.\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$. So, $y=-\\dfrac12x$ is an unstable line.\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y=-2x$.\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, $y=-2x$ is a stable line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is a saddle point.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/0e06d98a83065e6228e2e9238e34f00e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166476 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13af6d27-628d-5bdb-b396-299d9cfd64e7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Parameters of a System Given Its Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "986f9190-9e73-5e08-b08d-ec779933b0cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-21635" :question/problem "The phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_1 = [-1, \\ 1]^T$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2 = [1, \\ 0]^T$, respectively.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/7f0b26ad72c30f42ceeabc3bf1096678.png)\n\nIdentify the relations between $\\lambda_1$, $\\lambda_2$, and $0$." :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= -x$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/3f9d87b005f31a0a928364a190e4d763.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_1 > 0$.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation\n$y= 0$,\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3239/Source/Images/a75b5bab1a5310e66dfd91a6e1bae33a.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_2 > 0$.\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is $\\lambda_2$.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda_2 > \\lambda_1 > 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "35492718-d2d7-5c9d-9c02-1ef6ae0445d0" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Distinct Real Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "815b8138-f510-5107-a4e5-02ac4eea8d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164462} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6bf31d-ae8a-5604-a0a6-3472491c8dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable node (source)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164463} {:answer/id #uuid "2af9f5cc-d483-5244-9711-58ebfc066520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable node (sink)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164464}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4c563d-8027-50b1-913d-cbfe46b3b03f" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164461} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84c293e8-e637-5ce6-b268-3a68429c50c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164466} {:answer/id #uuid "1f131eb7-cf46-5bfe-a9bc-ea9c8d3feea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164467} {:answer/id #uuid "224fc0f4-09e8-5a41-8ec6-c56e757c640e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = − x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164468} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0288de-0ab0-5675-938e-54ef6c4ab8ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164469}] :answer-field/id #uuid "77950033-a58b-530b-81aa-1b6baa7ed3f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164465} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdf768f1-d157-594e-93e6-8712d929efef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164471} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb8ccc9-51f2-5dd2-bb26-551b5a471cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164472}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f60034b7-9188-55cc-a8b2-57dccf7ab523" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164470} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84bedda-2559-5487-9e67-150250512458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "a stable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164474} {:answer/id #uuid "4f5e11be-145e-5998-9889-0c945d104df0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "an unstable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164475}] :answer-field/id #uuid "63823202-0c50-5533-9e21-f95eba077a8f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186164473}] :question/id #uuid "f735f61a-0791-5bf2-b5e1-a997e0f18859" :question/math-academy-id "q-333646" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-3x(t) - 2y(t); y^{′}(t) =-x(t) - 2y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ_{1} =-4$ and $λ_{2} =-1$, for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $v_{1} = [2, 1]^{T}$ and $v_{2} = [- 1, 1]^{T}$, respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.\n$y = \\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}."}]}
